# core/graph-provenance - Where did each GRAPH come from?
#
# Answers: for every named graph the conversion described, what produced it, from
# which source, and when. One row per graph.
#
# Does NOT prove: that the graph is complete, or that its source is current. A
# bundle records how the graph came to exist, not whether the architecture in it
# was true at that moment.
#
# The difference from core/provenance is the SUBJECT. core/provenance traces the
# model behind one element; this traces the graphs themselves. Both are needed:
# a dataset can hold a graph whose model-level provenance looks fine while the
# graph was generated by a run nobody can account for, and only this shows that.
#
# The bundle's subject IS the graph IRI - `<...>/graph/model a prov:Bundle` - which
# is what makes this answerable at all. The converter emits one per graph it writes
# and puts it in the provenance graph, so this reads the provenance role and gets
# statements about the semantic, model and views graphs alongside it.
#
# Every field but the graph itself is OPTIONAL, and deliberately: a bundle that
# records only a timestamp is still worth a row, because "this graph exists and
# nothing says what made it" is the finding. A required pattern here would hide
# exactly the bundles worth looking at.
#
# ?source is the source RECORD, not a filename. Follow it with core/provenance for
# the repository, path and digest behind it.
#
# GRAPH_MATCH narrows to the graphs whose IRI contains a fragment - a notation slug
# ("bpmn"), a model id ("order-fulfillment"), or a graph role ("graph/semantic").
# Read the slug off a graph IRI rather than assuming it: the minted form is
# {base}{notation}/{modelId}/graph/{role} and the notation segment is the converter's,
# so ArchiMate output sits under `model/` rather than `archimate/`.
#
# Default is every graph, which is the right answer to "account for this dataset" and
# the wrong one to "where did this one model come from". It matters because a converter may
# partition a semantic graph per input file: one dataset answers the second question
# with 100 rows of which 97 are per-catalog-file partitions of a notation nobody
# asked about. Provenance is meant to be attached to every answer, so paying that
# once per answer is what makes people stop attaching it.
#
# Matching on the IRI rather than on a notation role is deliberate. The bundle's
# subject is the graph, and the graph IRI is the only thing in these statements that
# carries the notation - the bundle does not restate it, so there is no role to ask
# for. That also makes GRAPH_MATCH work for the model id and the role, which a
# notation parameter could not.
#
# Parameters: GRAPH_MATCH, LIMIT
{{PREFIXES}}
SELECT ?graph ?generated ?source ?activity ?agent
WHERE {
  {{GRAPH_OPEN:provenance}}
    ?graph a {{ROLE:bundle_class}} .
    FILTER({{GRAPH_MATCH}} = "" || CONTAINS(STR(?graph), {{GRAPH_MATCH}}))
    OPTIONAL { ?graph {{ROLE:generated_at}} ?generated }
    OPTIONAL { ?graph {{ROLE:derived_from}} ?source }
    OPTIONAL {
      ?graph {{ROLE:generated_by}} ?activity .
      OPTIONAL { ?activity prov:wasAssociatedWith ?agent }
    }
  {{GRAPH_CLOSE}}
}
ORDER BY ?graph
LIMIT {{LIMIT}}
